    "ex-srswor-inclusion-probabilities-and-negative-covariance": {
      "risk": "low",
      "kind": "example",
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-finite-population-and-sampling-design",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $N\\ge1$ be an integer, let $U=\\{1,\\ldots,N\\}$, and let\n$y:U\\to\\mathbb R$ be a fixed function. The pair $(U,y)$ is a **finite\npopulation**; its **total** and **mean** are\n$$T(y):=\\sum_{i\\in U}y_i,\\qquad \\bar y_U:=T(y)/N.$$\n\nA **sampling design** on $U$ is a nonempty finite set\n$\\mathcal S_d\\subseteq\\mathcal P(U)$ and a function\n$p_d:\\mathcal S_d\\to(0,1]$ with $\\sum_{s\\in\\mathcal S_d}p_d(s)=1$.\nHere $\\mathcal S_d$ is the support of the design law: zero-weight subsets are\nomitted. Thus $(\\mathcal S_d,p_d)$ is a finite probability space in the sense\nof [[def-finite-probability-space-and-event]]. For $A\\subseteq\\mathcal S_d$\nand $g:\\mathcal S_d\\to\\mathbb R$, put\n$$P_d(A):=\\sum_{s\\in A}p_d(s),\\qquad E_d[g]:=\\sum_{s\\in\\mathcal S_d}p_d(s)g(s).$$\nThe random sample is the subset $S\\in\\mathcal S_d$; probability and\nexpectation here average over the design while holding every $y_i$ fixed.\n\nFor $i,j\\in U$, define the **inclusion indicator**\n$$I_i(s):=\\begin{cases}1,&i\\in s,\\\\0,&i\\notin s,\\end{cases}$$\nthe **first-order inclusion probability**\n$$\\pi_i:=P_d(\\{s\\in\\mathcal S_d:i\\in s\\}),$$\nand the **second-order inclusion probability**\n$$\\pi_{ij}:=P_d(\\{s\\in\\mathcal S_d:i,j\\in s\\}).$$\nIn particular, $\\pi_{ii}=\\pi_i$.\n\nThe observed record includes the selected unit labels and their values. For a\ncommon record space set\n$$\\mathcal X_d:=\\{(s,z):s\\in\\mathcal S_d,\\ z:s\\to\\mathbb R\\},\\qquad \\mathcal A_d:=\\mathcal P(\\mathcal X_d),$$\nand for each $y\\in\\mathbb R^U$ define\n$$O_y(s):=(s,y|_s),\\qquad P_y^d(B):=\\sum_{\\substack{s\\in\\mathcal S_d\\\\O_y(s)\\in B}}p_d(s)\\quad(B\\in\\mathcal A_d).$$\nFor a real-valued statistic $h$ on $\\mathcal X_d$, write\n$$E_y^d[h]:=\\sum_{s\\in\\mathcal S_d}p_d(s)h(O_y(s))=E_d[h\\circ O_y];$$\nthis is expectation under the induced observed-data law.\nThe local well-definedness result\n[[lem-finite-population-design-law-is-a-probability-measure]] shows that each\n$P_y^d$ is a probability measure on $(\\mathcal X_d,\\mathcal A_d)$. Hence\n$$\\mathsf E_d:=((\\mathcal X_d,\\mathcal A_d),\\mathbb R^U,(P_y^d)_{y\\in\\mathbb R^U})$$\nis a statistical experiment ([[def-statistical-experiment]]), with parametric\nmodel $\\mathsf P_d(y)=P_y^d$ ([[def-parametric-statistical-model]]). The total\n$y\\mapsto T(y)$ and mean $y\\mapsto\\bar y_U$ are estimands\n([[def-estimand-on-a-statistical-model]]).\n\nThis is design probability: the $y_i$ are fixed and only $S$ is random. A\nsuperpopulation model instead assigns a probability law to the values $y_i$;\nthat additional law is separate from $p_d$. The finite-population target and\nthe sampling unit must be recorded in the scientific model contract\n([[def-scientific-statistical-model-contract]]). In fixed-design regression,\nthe matrix $X$ is held fixed and has no selection law; that convention alone\ndoes not specify a sampling design on $U$\n([[def-fixed-design-linear-regression-model]]).",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-horvitz-thompson-total-is-design-unbiased",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(U,y)$ be a finite population and let $p_d$ be a sampling design as in\n[[def-finite-population-and-sampling-design]]. Suppose $\\pi_i>0$ for every\n$i\\in U$. Give the real target space its Borel sigma-algebra. For an observed\nrecord $(s,z)\\in\\mathcal X_d$, define\n$$\\widehat T_{HT}(s,z):=\\sum_{i\\in s}\\frac{z_i}{\\pi_i},\\qquad \\widehat{\\bar y}_{HT}(s,z):=\\frac{\\widehat T_{HT}(s,z)}{N}.$$\nUnder the induced observed-data law $P_y^d$, these are estimators of the\nfinite-population total and mean, respectively, and\n$$E_y^d[\\widehat T_{HT}]=T(y),\\qquad E_y^d[\\widehat{\\bar y}_{HT}]=\\bar y_U.$$",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-variance-and-covariance",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For real random variables $X$ and $Y$ on one finite probability space, define\n$$\\operatorname{Var}(X):=\\mathbb E[(X-\\mathbb E[X])^2],\\qquad \\operatorname{Cov}(X,Y):=\\mathbb E[(X-\\mathbb E[X])(Y-\\mathbb E[Y])].$$\nThe **standard deviation** of $X$ is $\\sigma_X:=\\operatorname{Var}(X)^{1/2}$, using the unique nonnegative square root supplied by [[thm-of-square-roots]]. Variance is nonnegative because its defining random variable is pointwise nonnegative and expectation is a sum with nonnegative weights.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-linearity-of-expectation",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For a finite family of real random variables $(X_i)_{i\\in I}$ on one finite probability space and real scalars $(a_i)_{i\\in I}$,\n$$\\mathbb E\\!\\left[\\sum_{i\\in I}a_iX_i\\right]=\\sum_{i\\in I}a_i\\mathbb E[X_i].$$\nNo independence hypothesis is required. For $I=\\varnothing$, both sides are $0$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "lem-indicator-expectation-and-products",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For every event $A$, $\\mathbb E[\\mathbf1_A]=\\mathbb P(A)$. For every finite family $(A_i)_{i\\in I}$,\n$$\\prod_{i\\in I}\\mathbf1_{A_i}=\\mathbf1_{\\cap_{i\\in I}A_i},$$\nand $\\sum_{i\\in I}\\mathbf1_{A_i}(\\omega)$ is the number of events $A_i$ that contain $\\omega$. The empty product is $1$ and the empty sum is $0$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "def-indicator-random-variable",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For an event $A$ in a finite probability space, its **indicator random variable** is\n$$\\mathbf 1_A(\\omega):=\\begin{cases}1,&\\omega\\in A,\\\\0,&\\omega\\notin A.\\end{cases}$$",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "def-expectation-on-a-finite-probability-space",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **expectation** of a real random variable $X$ on a finite probability space $(\\Omega,w)$ is\n$$\\mathbb E[X]:=\\sum_{\\omega\\in\\Omega}X(\\omega)w(\\omega).$$\nFor a real constant $c$, the notation $c$ also denotes the constant random variable $\\omega\\mapsto c$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [],
      "routine_steps": [
        {
          "id": "count-first-order-inclusion",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "Let $a$ be the number of $n$-subsets containing a fixed unit. A permutation of $U$ taking one unit to another gives a bijection of the sample family, so every unit occurs in exactly $a$ samples. Counting pairs $(s,i)$ with $i\\in s$ in two ways gives $Na=nM$, hence $a/M=n/N$ and $\\pi_i=n/N$.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "construct",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "count-second-order-inclusion",
          "step": "1.2",
          "claim": "Let $b$ be the number of samples containing a fixed ordered pair of distinct units. Permutations of $U$ give the same $b$ for every such pair. Counting triples $(s,i,j)$ with $i\\ne j$ and $i,j\\in s$ gives $N(N-1)b=n(n-1)M$, so $b/M=n(n-1)/(N(N-1))$ and $\\pi_{ij}=n(n-1)/(N(N-1))$. This also gives $\\pi_{ij}=0$ when $n=1$.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "construct",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derive-inclusion-covariance",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "Put $A_i=\\{s:i\\in s\\}$. By [F5] and [F6], $E_d[I_i]=P_d(A_i)=\\pi_i$ and $E_d[I_iI_j]=P_d(A_i\\cap A_j)=\\pi_{ij}$. Expanding the covariance in [F3] with linearity [F4] therefore gives $\\operatorname{Cov}_d(I_i,I_j)=\\pi_{ij}-\\pi_i\\pi_j=\\frac{n(n-1)}{N(N-1)}-\\frac{n^2}{N^2}=-\\frac{n(N-n)}{N^2(N-1)}$.",
          "inputs": [
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "F6",
            "F7",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 1.2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "recover-srs-sample-mean",
          "step": "3.1",
          "claim": "Since $\\pi_i=n/N>0$, the estimator in [F2] has $\\widehat{\\bar y}_{HT}(s,y|_s)=\\frac1N\\sum_{i\\in s}\\frac{y_i}{n/N}=\\frac1n\\sum_{i\\in s}y_i$. Its expectation is $\\bar y_U$ by [F2].",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The example assumes N>=2 and 1<=n<=N."
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Step 1.2 covers pi_ij=0 when n=1; step 2.1 still gives the correct negative covariance."
        },
        {
          "case": "one",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Steps 1.1–2.1 include the singleton sample-size case n=1."
        },
        {
          "case": "degenerate",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "When n=N, step 2.1 gives zero covariance for the census design."
        },
        {
          "case": "endpoints",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "No interval parameter or endpoint appears in this finite counting example."
        },
        {
          "case": "nonempty-choice",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "All sample families are finite and counted explicitly; no arbitrary choice is used."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-forward",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The example states direct formulas, not an equivalence."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-reverse",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The example states direct formulas, not an equivalence."
        }
      ],
      "finite_smoke": []
    },
